The linked article explains why the author likes the term 'clanker' and emphasizes that machines are not people.

In 1940, all of America fell in love with a talking cardboard cricket.

@badlogicgames discusses the concept of 'model wellbeing' and suggests that current models are just large matrix multiplications. He argues that sentience is likely substrate independent, meaning it doesn't depend on the physical substrate but on certain functional qualities. A model alone isn't conscious or sentient because it lacks features like continuity, self-maintenance, belief consolidation, embodiment, grounding, and feedback loops. Adding persistence, feedback, and sensors can help close some gaps toward machine sentience, but many more boxes need to be checked before we should worry about model wellbeing.

The linked article from Boston Review states that within the next decade, systems that are serious candidates for consciousness may emerge.

People are now token-maximizing to make Dario and Sama happy.

@micLivs introduces pi-dynamic-workflows, which is likely to be a bigger token burner than pi-goal. It is the first implementation of subagents that the author doesn't dislike, mainly because it provides a 'code mode' for subagents. The agent writes a JavaScript-based workflow DSL into a dedicated tool, which the engine then parses and runs.

The DSL includes primitives like agent(), parallel(), pipeline(), phase(), and log() to keep things simple. The feature is now available in @badlogicgames Pi, which can be installed via npm. The video demonstrates how it works.

Recommended reading. Entirely unsurprisingly, Vicki also has good takes on agentic coding.

